#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# ceiling

#% text
# `ceiling(x)` gives the least integer at least `x`.

#% text
# `ceiling(x, a)` gives the least multiple of `a` at least `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact numbers, machine numbers and balls are rounded; exact constants such as `pi` are rounded by a numerical bound.

#% item
# For complex numbers the real and imaginary parts are rounded separately.

#% item
# `ceiling` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
ceiling(2.3)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
ceiling(-2.3)
#% output
#= -2

#% cell
ceiling(7/2)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
ceiling(pi)
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The next multiple of 5:

#% cell
ceiling(17, 5)
#% output
#= 20

#% text
# A complex number:

#% cell
ceiling(1.2 - 3.7*im)
#% output
#= 2 - 3im

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
ceiling([0.1, 1.0, 1.9])
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `ceiling(x)` is `-floor(-x)`:

#% cell
[ceiling(-4.5), -floor(4.5)]
#% output
#= [-4, -4]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `floor`, `round`, `integer_part`, `quotient`
